      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the constraints provided: no two hexagons with a common side can be colored the same color. This means that each new hexagon connected to an existing colored hexagon must have a different color.\n\nFirst, we start with the red hexagon. Since this is the first hexagon, we can choose any color for the other three hexagons that are not connected to it. There are 3 choices for the color of the hexagons that are adjacent to the red hexagon (yellow or green).\n\nAfter coloring these three hexagons, we have 2 remaining colors (the original color of the red hexagon and one of the colors previously chosen for the adjacent hexagons). Therefore, we have 2 choices for the color of each hexagon created by connecting to these three hexagons.\n\nWe can represent the pattern formed by each choice of colors. Let's illustrate the pattern with a diagram of the initial red hexagon (R):\n\n   R ---------- Y ----> J ----> H\n    |        |           |\n    --------------------------\n   E ---------- G ----> B ----> C\n\nFrom this diagram, we can see that each colored line (hypotenuse of the diagram) represents a new hexagon, and the red hexagon (R) goes first. The other three lines (Y, G, H) and the two new hexagons (B, C) can be colored.\n\n1. We have 3 colors to choose from for the hexagon adjacent to the red hexagon.\n2. There are 2 choices for the hexagon adjacent to the hexagon adjacent to the red hexagon (either one color of the original red hexagon or one of the 2 remaining colors).\n3. There are 2 choices for the hexagon adjacent to the two new hexagons (either one color of the original red hexagon or one of the 2 remaining colors).\n\nThus, the total number of ways to color the figure is $3 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 12$.",
